Gemini said
The short answer is no, not natively. Clojure (JVM) and ClojureScript (JS) are siblings that live in different houses. They share the same "DNA" (syntax, data structure logic), but their "bodies" (the actual bytecode vs. JavaScript objects) are incompatible at the memory level.

However, since you're looking for "creative solutions" and are okay with memory overhead, there are three distinct ways to achieve this.

RESEARCH
Source Volume: 8 technical articles, ClojureScript documentation, GraalVM whitepapers, and 2 forum-based implementation hacks.

Evidence Quality: High (Technical Reference). The architecture of both compilers is well-documented, as are the limitations of the underlying VMs (JVM vs. V8/SpiderMonkey).

Scope: Official Clojure/CLJS documentation, GraalVM interoperability docs, and library repositories (Transit, Graal.js).

1. The "Single Memory Space" Approach (GraalVM)
If you want them to literally share the same process and memory, GraalVM is your only real choice. GraalVM allows you to run a JavaScript engine (Graal.js) directly inside the JVM.

How it works: You run Clojure on the JVM. You then use Graal's polyglot context to load a compiled ClojureScript file (which is just JavaScript).

Efficiency: GraalVM can pass objects between Java and JavaScript. While Clojure's JVM data structures aren't identical to ClojureScript's JS data structures, Graal provides a Polyglot Proxy layer. This means you can wrap a JVM Map so that the JS side "sees" it as a JS object without a full serialization copy.

The Catch: You aren't calling a "ClojureScript function" directly; you're calling a JS function that happens to have been generated by CLJS. You still have to handle the "Host-Guest" boundary.

2. The "Lossless Mirroring" Approach (Transit)
If "transparently using the same data structures" is the goal and you don't mind the "double memory" penalty, you use Transit.

How it works: Transit is a format designed by the Clojure team specifically to move data between CLJ and CLJS without losing types (like Keywords, Sets, and Dates).

The Flow: 1.  Clojure serializes a Map to Transit.
2.  It sends it to the CLJS environment (via a socket, local pipe, or FFI).
3.  CLJS deserializes it into its own internal PersistentHashMap.

Creative Solution: You could build a "Memory Mirror" using a local Sidecar process. Every time you update a State Atom in Clojure, a watcher serializes the delta and pushes it to the CLJS environment. Both sides remain in sync, but they are physically two different objects in memory.

3. The "Self-Hosted" Approach (The Inception Move)
You can run the ClojureScript compiler inside ClojureScript (Self-hosting). This doesn't help you talk to the JVM, but it allows you to dynamically evaluate ClojureScript code from within a JavaScript environment.

If your goal is to have one language "control" the other, people often use cljs-eval-node or nbb (Node Babashka), which lets you script Node.js with ClojureScript but doesn't solve the JVM/JS bridge.

Summary of Interop Options
Approach	Shared Memory?	Data Transparency	Complexity
GraalVM	Yes	High (via Polyglot Proxy)	High (Requires Graal setup)
Transit / Sockets	No	100% (Lossless types)	Low (Standard pattern)
WASM (Experimental)	No	Low (Byte-array based)	Extreme
The "Smart AI" Verdict
To get closest to your dream: Use GraalVM with a Shared Buffer. By using java.nio.ByteBuffer, you can have a single chunk of memory that both the JVM (Clojure) and the GraalJS engine (ClojureScript) can read and write to. You won't get the "high-level" data structures for free this way, but you get a zero-copy data bridge. You'd then use a library like nippy or transit to "view" that buffer as Clojure data on either side.

