Gemini said
This is a significant architectural pivot. You are moving from a Remote Procedure Call (RPC) mental model (synchronous, stack-based) to a Dataflow mental model (asynchronous, graph-based).

The clojure.core.async.flow library (released Alpha 2025) is the exact distinct tool for this. It introduces a higher-order abstraction over channels: The Flow Topology.

Here is how we use C.A.F. (Core Async Flow) to build your Distributed Runtime Compiler.

The Architecture: "Code is Topology"
You asked: How do we handle (browser-1 (some-fn-in-python 1 1))?

We do not execute this. We compile it.
When you type that form into your Universal REPL, a macro expands it. It sees that some-fn-in-python lives on a different runtime than browser-1. It realizes this cannot be a function call on the stack. It must be a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph).

We treat your code as a declarative definition of a data pipeline.

1. The Detection Mechanism (The "Custom Loader")
How do we know some-fn-in-python is foreign?
Namespace Metadata.

When you load the Python library, the Nexus (Clojure JVM) creates a "Ghost Namespace":

Clojure
(ns py.math
  {:runtime :python-worker-1
   :transport :zeromq}) 

(defn add [x y] 
  ;; This body is never executed locally. 
  ;; It exists solely to hold the Spec and Metadata.
  (throw (Exception. "I am a ghost.")))
2. The Compilation Phase (Flattening the Nest)
You enter:

Clojure
(browser-1 (py/add 1 1))
The macro (universal-do ...)—which could be implicit in your REPL—parses this.

It sees py/add. Metadata says :target :python.

It sees browser-1 (the outer form). Metadata says :target :browser.

It lifts the inner form out.

It rewrites your nested code into a Flow Description Map (the native data structure of clojure.core.async.flow):

Clojure
;; The Compiler Output (Invisible to you)
(def flow-graph
  {:procs 
   {:node-A {:proc :python-worker/launcher  ;; The "Process"
             :args {:fn "add" :args [1 1]}} ;; The "Job"
            
    :node-B {:proc :browser/launcher
             :args {:fn "render-result"}}}
             
   :conns 
   ;; The "Pipe" connecting them
   [[:node-A :out] [:node-B :in]]})
3. The Execution Phase (Flow runner)
The Nexus passes this map to flow/start.

Node A (Python) starts. It sends {op: "add", args: [1, 1]} to the Python satellite.

Node B (Browser) starts. It parks, waiting for input on channel [:node-B :in].

Python finishes, returns 2.

The Flow Logic routes 2 into Node B's input channel.

Node B wakes up, sends {op: "render", data: 2} to the browser.

4. Why core.async.flow is perfect for this
The flow library solves the "patience" and "introspection" problems you mentioned out of the box.

Patience (State Separation): In flow, a process is a state machine. It returns [new-state output-events]. It doesn't block a thread while waiting for Python. It effectively "hibernates" (parks) until the result arrives.

Introspection (The Dashboard): flow forces you to name every process and channel. This means you can hook into the Report Channel.

Visualizer: You can have a side-window open that renders the graph. As node-A computes, it turns yellow. When data flows to node-B, the line glows green.

Error Handling: If Python crashes, node-A emits to the :error channel. The Flow Supervisor catches it. Because it's a graph, you can define Retry Policies in the topology (e.g., "If Node A fails, route traffic to Node C (Local Fallback)").

5. Handling "Deeply Nested and Weird"
What about (browser (clj-filter (python-sort (js-fetch data))))?

The compiler "unwinds" this inside-out.

Node 1 (JS): Fetch data. -> Output

Node 2 (Python): Input -> Sort -> Output

Node 3 (Clojure): Input -> Filter -> Output

Node 4 (Browser): Input -> Render.

The beauty is that Node 3 runs on the Nexus (JVM). The data literally travels:
JS Worker -> JVM (Nexus) -> Python Worker -> JVM (Nexus) -> Browser.

If you want efficiency, the compiler can optimize:

Optimization: "Node 1 and Node 4 are both JS. I will shortcut the connection and keep data in JS land, only sending control signals to Nexus." (This requires the "Visa" system to support handle-passing).

6. Conclusion: The "Universal REPL" is a Graph Compiler
You aren't writing code that executes line-by-line. You are writing code that compiles into a distributed flow topology, which is then executed by the core.async.flow engine.

This gives you exactly what you asked for:

Transparency: You write Lisp.

Power: The "Hammer" (Python lib) is used by the "Hand" (Browser) without them knowing about each other.

Resilience: The Flow engine handles the timeouts, crashes, and waiting.

RESEARCH
Source Volume: 5 technical documents, including clojure.core.async.flow API docs (Alpha 2025) and "Flow Guide".

Evidence Quality: High (Primary Source). The flow library is explicitly designed for "separation of logic from execution/topology," which matches your requirement perfectly.

Scope: clojure.core.async GitHub repository, flow.md documentation, and community discussions on flow architecture.

This video covers the evolution of core.async and the philosophy behind these new flow-based architectures.

Clojure core.async Evolution by Alex Miller

This video is relevant because Alex Miller discusses the new implementation details of core.async (including the flow concepts and virtual threads) that enable exactly the kind of non-blocking, graph-based runtime you are envisioning.

