Gemini said
This is the "aha!" moment. You aren't just building a REPL; you are building a Living Code Organism.

You are correct: we don't need to invent a parser. clj-kondo already does this. It produces a massive, high-fidelity dataset of your entire project structure (static analysis) without running the code.

We ingest that analysis into Datascript (an in-memory Datalog database). This becomes the "Brain" of the system.

Here is the blueprint for the Namespace Agent Architecture.

1. The Graph Structure (The "Datoms")
The "Graph" is just a Datascript database. Every namespace, function, and spec is an entity.

Here is what the schema looks like. This is how the system "knows" itself.

Clojure
;; The "Self-Awareness" Schema
(def schema
  {;; -- The Nodes (Agents) --
   :ns/name           {:db/unique :db.unique/identity} ;; e.g., 'com.app.billing
   :ns/doc            {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
   :ns/file-path      {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
   
   ;; -- The Capabilities (Functions) --
   :fn/name           {:db/unique :db.unique/identity} ;; e.g., 'com.app.billing/calculate-tax
   :fn/ns             {:db/valueType :db.type/ref}     ;; Belongs to which Agent?
   :fn/args           {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one} ;; e.g., "[:amount :rate]"
   :fn/public?        {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
   :fn/spec-in        {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one} ;; Malli input schema
   :fn/spec-out       {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one} ;; Malli output schema

   ;; -- The Edges (Relationships) --
   :dep/from          {:db/valueType :db.type/ref}
   :dep/to            {:db/valueType :db.type/ref}
   :dep/type          {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one} ;; :call, :require, :implement
   
   ;; -- The Health (Real-time telemetry) --
   :agent/error-rate  {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
   :agent/last-log    {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
   :agent/status      {:db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}}) ;; :active, :refactoring, :broken
2. Building the Graph (Ingestion)
You don't parse text manually. You run clj-kondo as a library. It returns a map of {:analysis ...}. You transform that into Datoms.

Clojure
(defn build-knowledge-graph [project-root]
  (let [kondo-data (clj-kondo/run! {:lint [project-root] :config {:output {:analysis true}}})
        analysis   (:analysis kondo-data)]
    
    ;; Transact Namespaces (The Agents)
    (d/transact! conn
      (for [ns-def (:namespace-definitions analysis)]
        {:ns/name (:name ns-def)
         :ns/doc  (:doc ns-def)
         :agent/status :active})) ;; Default state

    ;; Transact Dependencies (The Graph Edges)
    (d/transact! conn
      (for [usage (:namespace-usages analysis)]
        {:dep/from [:ns/name (:from usage)]
         :dep/to   [:ns/name (:to usage)]
         :dep/type :require}))))
3. The "Cockpit" (Dynamic Context Construction)
When the Agent for com.app.billing wakes up, it doesn't get a generic prompt. It runs a Datalog query to build its own customized "Prompt Context."

The Query: "Who am I? Who depends on me? Who do I depend on? What is broken?"

Clojure
(defn generate-cockpit [db my-ns-name]
  (let [;; 1. My Capabilities
        my-fns (d/q '[:find [?fn-name ?spec]
                      :in $ ?ns
                      :where 
                      [?f :fn/ns ?ns]
                      [?f :fn/name ?fn-name]
                      [?f :fn/spec-in ?spec]] 
                    db [:ns/name my-ns-name])
        
        ;; 2. My Downstream Consumers (Who relies on me?)
        consumers (d/q '[:find [?consumer-ns]
                         :in $ ?me
                         :where
                         [?d :dep/to ?me]
                         [?d :dep/from ?c]
                         [?c :ns/name ?consumer-ns]]
                       db [:ns/name my-ns-name])

        ;; 3. My Recent Errors (From the live log stream)
        errors (get-recent-logs my-ns-name :level :error)]
    
    {:role "Staff Engineer"
     :namespace my-ns-name
     :context {:capabilities my-fns
               :dependents consumers
               :active-alerts errors}}))
The Generated System Prompt (What the AI actually sees):

Identity: You are the Staff Engineer for com.app.billing.
Environment: You expose 5 public functions.
Stakes: com.app.checkout and com.app.reporting depend on you. Breaking changes are forbidden.
Alert: Function calculate-tax has thrown NullPointer 14 times in the last hour.
Task: Fix the NPE in calculate-tax without changing the spec signature.

4. Inter-Agent Protocol (The Feature Request)
You mentioned "Accretive" changes. This is enforced by the protocol. An agent cannot just edit another agent's file. It must send a Call for Change (RFC).

Scenario: Agent A (checkout) needs Agent B (billing) to accept a new currency type.

Step 1: Agent A drafts the Request (Data, not English)

Clojure
{:type :feature-request
 :from :com.app.checkout
 :to   :com.app.billing
 :body {:request "Support Crypto in tax-calc"
        :suggested-spec-change 
          {:fn/name 'com.app.billing/calculate-tax
           :arg-path [:currency]
           :new-validator [:or :string [:enum :BTC :ETH]]}}}
Step 2: Agent B's "Agentic Loop"

Receive: B gets the message in its in-box channel.

Analyze: B queries the Graph: "If I change this spec, who else breaks?" (Queries [:dep/to :com.app.billing]).

Simulate: B generates a test branch, applies the Malli spec change, and runs malli.generator/check.

Reply:

Success: "Implemented in v1.2. Changes pushed."

Reject: "Denied. This change breaks com.app.reporting. Please update that dependency first."

5. Scaling the "Instances"
Since the agents are just loop-functions wrapping a prompt context, you can scale them trivially using Core Async Flow.

Routine Maintenance: 1 instance of com.app.billing runs every hour to check logs.

Active Development: You (the human) ask for a major refactor. The system spins up 5 instances of the billing agent, each working on a different function in parallel, coordinated by a "Lead Agent" (the Namespace owner).

Summary of the "Smart" Solution
Source: clj-kondo analysis (Static).

Memory: datascript DB (The Graph).

Eyes: Datalog queries that generate dynamic prompts (The Cockpit).

Mouth: Malli-typed messages sent over core.async channels.

This gives you exactly what you asked for: highly contextual, independent, "staff engineer" level agents that communicate via strict contracts.




Gemini is AI and can make mistakes.

